/*!
    \example planet
    \ingroup quick3dphysics-examples
    \title Qt Quick 3D Physics - Planet Example
    \examplecategory {3D}
    \brief Demonstrates using custom gravity configuration for character controllers and rigid bodies.
    \image planet-example.webp

    The \e {Planet Example} demonstrates how to implement dynamic, point-centered spherical gravity for a \l CharacterController and \l DynamicRigidBody objects in \l {Qt Quick 3D Physics}.
    Instead of relying on a uniform downward gravity vector, a \l GravityField in \c Point mode
    applies a directional gravity force pointing toward the center of the planet, allowing the
    character to walk seamlessly around a 3D planetoid surface while physics objects fall toward its center.

    \section1 Overview

    In standard physics scenes, gravity is uniform and points along the negative Y-axis. For spherical worlds or micro-planets, the gravity vector must be calculated dynamically based on an object's position relative to the planet's center.

    This example highlights four main mechanics:
    \list
    \li Applying a dynamic gravity vector pointing toward the planet center with a \l GravityField.
    \li Orienting the \l CharacterController using its \c gravityOrientation property combined with player input.
    \li Pulling the cubes towards the center with the same \l GravityField as the character.
    \li Customizing the \l CharacterController slope limit, maximum step height, and sliding down steep slopes for curved geometry.
    \endlist

    The example also provides a control panel to adjust the gravity strength, the planet size,
    the number of cubes, the movement speed, the slope limit, the maximum step height, whether
    the character slides down steep slopes, and the mid-air control while the scene is running.

    \section1 Dynamic Gravity Calculation

    The global \l PhysicsWorld has no gravity. Instead, a \l GravityField in
    \c Point mode pulls the character controller and the cubes towards the
    center of the planet:

    \snippet planet/main.qml dynamic-gravity

    \section1 Surface Alignment and Rotation

    To keep the character upright relative to the spherical surface,
    its rotation incorporates the automatic alignment provided by \c gravityOrientation.

    When navigating spherical or complex curved surfaces, traditional approaches compute surface orientation from scratch each frame using cross-products against a fixed reference vector:
    \code
    QVector3D forward = {0, 0, -1};
    QVector3D right = QVector3D::crossProduct(forward, up).normalized();
    \endcode

    This classic method inevitably hits a geometric limitation as the character approaches the sphere poles.
    When the gravity vector aligns directly with the global reference axis, the vectors lose orthogonal stability,
    causing sudden snapping or unwanted spinning.

    To solve this singularity problem, the \c gravityOrientation property uses an \e {incremental delta-rotation} approach (parallel transport):
    \list
    \li It tracks the previous surface alignment vector derived from the local gravity field.
    \li As the character moves, it calculates the shortest arc rotation (\c QQuaternion::rotationTo) between the old gravity normal and the new gravity normal.
    \li This gravitational delta is incrementally accumulated, bypassing global world axes and preventing polar instability.
    \endlist

    In QML, this orientation is seamlessly combined with the player's runtime look direction:

    \snippet planet/main.qml character-orientation

    \section1 Dynamic Objects Spawning

    In addition to the character controller, dynamic cubic rigid bodies are spawned near the
    character's starting position. The \l GravityField pulls each of them towards the center of
    the planet, so the spawned cubes fall onto the surface. Changing the number of cubes or the
    size of the planet removes the cubes and spawns them again.

    \sa {Qt Quick 3D Physics}, CharacterController, PhysicsWorld, DynamicRigidBody
*/
